1
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:13,540
Welcome to one of the most important lessons in our course.

2
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,380
Today, we are going to master time travel.

3
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:24,520
To achieve a band 6.0 or higher in grammatical range and accuracy, you cannot stay stuck in the simple present.

4
00:00:25,060 --> 00:00:28,100
You need to show the examiner that you can move flexibly through

5
00:00:28,100 --> 00:00:34,000
time, discussing your past experiences, your current habits, and your future predictions with precision.

6
00:00:34,660 --> 00:00:38,060
In this lesson, we will cover the full spectrum, the core tenses,

7
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:42,360
how to handle the past, present, and the tricky, present perfect, 

8
00:00:42,360 --> 00:00:47,820
the future, how to discuss plans and make predictions without just saying will every time.

9
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:50,780
By the end of this video, you will have a toolkit of

10
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:55,080
structures to answer almost any question in parts one, two, or three.

11
00:00:55,540 --> 00:00:56,220
Let's get started.

12
00:01:00,410 --> 00:01:02,030
Let's start with the foundations.

13
00:01:02,910 --> 00:01:08,670
In IELTS speaking, if you aren't specifically asked about the past or future, your default setting is the simple present.

14
00:01:09,270 --> 00:01:11,530
We use this for most of our answers in part one.

15
00:01:11,870 --> 00:01:14,790
It covers facts, routines, hobbies, and opinions.

16
00:01:15,390 --> 00:01:16,210
I work as a teacher.

17
00:01:16,470 --> 00:01:19,030
I am a hardworking person, so I go to the office early.

18
00:01:19,830 --> 00:01:24,810
The form is subject plus verb, and you can use signal words like usually, often, or always.

19
00:01:24,810 --> 00:01:29,390
But what if the examiner asks about your childhood or a past holiday?

20
00:01:29,930 --> 00:01:32,150
Then you must switch to the simple past.

21
00:01:32,730 --> 00:01:34,750
The key here is that the time is definite.

22
00:01:35,070 --> 00:01:35,850
It is finished.

23
00:01:36,590 --> 00:01:37,630
I was a student back then.

24
00:01:37,910 --> 00:01:39,910
I enjoyed math, but I didn't like history.

25
00:01:40,570 --> 00:01:43,150
The form for this tense is subject plus verb, ed.

26
00:01:43,630 --> 00:01:46,630
Listen for keywords like last year or in your childhood.

27
00:01:46,970 --> 00:01:48,910
If you hear those, switch to the past immediately.

28
00:01:48,910 --> 00:01:52,850
Now, here is the secret weapon for Band 6 Plus, the present perfect.

29
00:01:53,390 --> 00:01:55,150
This tense connects the past to now.

30
00:01:55,650 --> 00:01:57,630
We use it for experiences up to this moment.

31
00:01:58,010 --> 00:01:58,690
Here is a comparison.

32
00:01:59,510 --> 00:01:59,950
Past simple.

33
00:02:00,550 --> 00:02:02,070
I lived in London in 2010.

34
00:02:02,750 --> 00:02:04,190
This means you already moved away.

35
00:02:05,370 --> 00:02:05,890
Present perfect.

36
00:02:07,150 --> 00:02:08,990
I have lived in London for two years.

37
00:02:09,830 --> 00:02:11,350
This means that you are still there.

38
00:02:12,170 --> 00:02:13,050
Here is a quick tip.

39
00:02:13,670 --> 00:02:15,130
Be careful with the word never.

40
00:02:15,730 --> 00:02:18,790
I never travel means you don't do it as a habit.

41
00:02:19,470 --> 00:02:22,870
I have never traveled means you haven't done it in your life until now.

42
00:02:23,490 --> 00:02:25,370
The best candidates mix these three together.

43
00:02:25,710 --> 00:02:28,110
For example, if asked, how has education changed?

44
00:02:28,470 --> 00:02:30,890
You can say education has changed significantly.

45
00:02:31,470 --> 00:02:33,370
In the past, it focused on theory.

46
00:02:33,730 --> 00:02:35,670
However, now it focuses on practice.

47
00:02:36,370 --> 00:02:38,410
This mix shows total control of grammar.

48
00:02:42,510 --> 00:02:45,630
Now that we've covered the past and present, let's jump to the future.

49
00:02:45,630 --> 00:02:49,210
In speaking part one or two, you are often asked about your plans.

50
00:02:49,670 --> 00:02:50,770
Don't just overuse will.

51
00:02:51,250 --> 00:02:52,270
We have better options.

52
00:02:53,050 --> 00:02:55,790
First, let's distinguish between will and going to.

53
00:02:56,590 --> 00:02:58,810
Will is usually for decisions made right now.

54
00:03:00,210 --> 00:03:00,310
Examiner.

55
00:03:00,870 --> 00:03:02,290
What are you doing after the test?

56
00:03:03,190 --> 00:03:03,410
You.

57
00:03:04,210 --> 00:03:05,630
I think I will go home and sleep.

58
00:03:06,530 --> 00:03:08,910
Going to is for plans you have already thought about.

59
00:03:09,510 --> 00:03:12,590
For example, I am going to meet my friend for dinner.

60
00:03:13,190 --> 00:03:17,210
Using going to for your life plan sounds much more natural.

61
00:03:17,870 --> 00:03:23,550
To sound more formal and academic, swap going to for plan to or intend to.

62
00:03:23,790 --> 00:03:24,670
Here is an example.

63
00:03:25,250 --> 00:03:26,870
I intend to start my own business.

64
00:03:27,750 --> 00:03:32,830
Notice we don't say in the future here because the word intend already implies the future.

65
00:03:33,230 --> 00:03:35,410
It's clean, precise and high scoring.

66
00:03:36,190 --> 00:03:37,050
What if you aren't sure?

67
00:03:37,630 --> 00:03:38,690
What if it's just a dream?

68
00:03:39,270 --> 00:03:40,650
Use modals like could or would.

69
00:03:40,650 --> 00:03:43,050
I could change careers, but I'm not sure.

70
00:03:43,830 --> 00:03:45,610
I would love to travel if I had the money.

71
00:03:46,490 --> 00:03:49,210
These are great for hypothetical questions in part two.

72
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:57,540
Finally, let's tackle the most difficult part, predictions.

73
00:03:58,380 --> 00:04:03,500
In speaking part three, the examiner might ask, do you think robots will replace teachers?

74
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:06,220
You need to show how certain you are.

75
00:04:06,440 --> 00:04:08,800
For that, let's look at the scale of certainty.

76
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:13,620
If you see a strong trend and want to sound objective, use the expected to.

77
00:04:14,180 --> 00:04:18,200
For example, technology is expected to play a huge role in education.

78
00:04:18,899 --> 00:04:22,560
This sounds much more professional than just saying technology will be important.

79
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:25,500
If something is probable, use be likely to.

80
00:04:25,980 --> 00:04:27,480
This is a favorite of native speakers.

81
00:04:27,820 --> 00:04:33,140
For example, cars are likely to become electric in the next decade, or you can use it in the negative form.

82
00:04:33,140 --> 00:04:37,280
Robots aren't likely to replace human creativity, and if you are guessing,

83
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:39,960
go back to our modals, may or could.

84
00:04:40,380 --> 00:04:42,080
People may work from home more often.

85
00:04:42,580 --> 00:04:51,100
Space travel could become common. Using this range, from expected to down to could, is exactly what the examiner looks for in grammatical range.

86
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:57,520
So let's recap our time travel toolkit.

87
00:04:58,080 --> 00:05:02,320
For the past, use simple past for finished events and present perfect for experiences.

88
00:05:02,320 --> 00:05:05,440
For the present, stick to simple present for facts.

89
00:05:06,120 --> 00:05:11,280
For future plans, use intend to or going to for real plans and will for quick decisions.

90
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:16,600
For predictions, use likely to or expected to to sound academic.

91
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:22,550
Master this flexibility and you will be well on your way to that band 6.0+.

92
00:05:22,550 --> 00:05:25,000
Thanks for watching and see you in the next lesson.

